{"name":"Foggy Amethyst","hex":"#57317e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foggy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Obscured by mist or fog; unclear; hazy.","Confused, befuddled, or vague."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foggy"},{"term":"amethyst","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A transparent purple to violet variety of quartz with traces of manganese, used as a gemstone.","A purple colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour similar to that of the gemstone"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amethyst"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog"},{"title":"Amethyst","blocks":[{"text":"Amethyst is a violet variety of natural quartz. Ancient Greeks wore amethyst and carved drinking vessels from it in the belief that it would prevent intoxication. Amethyst, a semiprecious stone, is often used in jewelry. It occurs mostly in association with calcite, quartz, smoky quartz, hematite, pyrite, fluorite, goethite, agate, and chalcedony."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amethyst"},{"title":"Lavender (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lavender is a light shade of purple or violet. It applies particularly to the color of the flower of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Lavandula_fields.jpg/330px-Lavandula_fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavender_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Minute droplets of water constitute this after-dark radiation fog, with an ambient temperature of −2 °C (28 °F). Their motion trails are captured as streaks.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of water droplets forming fog. Those outside the camera lens's depth of field appear as orbs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Fri